WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:07.390
In the social media age, we’ve all had to
figure out how to deal with weird, unwanted

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:08.700
messages.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:14.110
We can block the sender on Twitter, delete
them from Facebook, or just chalk it up to

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:20.750
experience and never “swipe right” on
someone with a Bill Cosby tattoo ever again.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:25.900
But what if the messages you’re receiving
aren’t from some random troll or probable

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:30.730
catfish, but a malevolent presence straight
from the ninth circle of Hell?

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:37.660
If you’re planning to contact the dead,
then the Ouija Board is probably your best

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:38.660
option.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:44.420
But these things don’t tend to come preinstalled
with a convenient ‘block’ button in case

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:50.850
you fail to get through to your recently deceased
great uncle Albert and instead find yourself

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:58.860
on a private line with Abraxas, Great Archon
and demonic princeps of the 365 spheres.

00:00:58.860 --> 00:00:59.890
Awkward.

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:05.740
Instead, each Ouija Board is marked with the
letters of the alphabet, numbers from one

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:12.020
to nine, and sometimes the words “Yes”,
“No”, “Hello” and “Goodbye” ... presumably

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:17.520
as a courtesy to any antisocial demons or
introverted spirits who simply aren’t in

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:18.790
a talkative mood.

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:24.240
The players – or flesh and blood instruments
of evil, if you prefer – place their fingers

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:29.991
on a wooden or plastic pointer in the centre
of the board called a “planchette”, which

00:01:29.991 --> 00:01:35.630
then moves around as if with a will of its
own, hopefully spelling out messages from

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:38.300
the other side in the process.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:44.900
The name ‘Ouija Board’ is actually a trademark
of the toy company hasbro, which seems like

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:51.369
kind of a strange fit - bright and breezy
toys aren’t obvious shelf-mates for mysterious

00:01:51.369 --> 00:01:53.540
occult objects after all.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:02:00.050
But Ouija Boards haven’t always had their
present day kinda-controversial reputation.

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:06.860
‘Spirit boards’ or ‘talking boards’
have been around since the Victorian era.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:13.170
And the trend of effectively saying ‘DM
me, babe’ to the dead is even older than

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:14.170
that.

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:18.370
One of the first mentions of this kind of
“automatic writing” -- as it would later

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:24.489
be known -- comes from China as far back as
eleven hundred AD, and historians have uncovered

00:02:24.489 --> 00:02:30.000
similar practices in India, Greece, Rome,
and medieval Europe.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.790
It’s in nineteenth century America, however,
that the story of the modern-day Ouija Board

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:39.620
really begins.

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:45.510
Spiritualism had been a big deal in Europe
for years by then, but it was in 1848 in upstate

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:49.540
New York, that the New World suddenly started
to take notice.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:55.560
This was in large part due to the Fox Sisters”,
who claimed they were being haunted by a spirit

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:02.620
called “Mr Splitfoot”, who would communicate
with them via mysterious raps and knocks.

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:07.550
The Fox sisters’ story captivated the public,
and they became the first spiritualists to

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:10.709
perform for a paying audience.

00:03:10.709 --> 00:03:16.480
They would demonstrate something called “table
turning”, where gullible folks would place

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:22.720
their hands on a small table, only for it
to start moving beneath them seemingly of

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:27.190
it’s own volition, as well as automatic
writing exhibitions, spelling out messages

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:28.940
from the spirit world.

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:33.170
The act was wildly popular, and the sisters
even went on tour.

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:37.980
It was basically Beatlemania, but with a lot
more ectoplasm.

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:43.480
Stories about spiritualism were all over the
newspapers, and in 1886 the Associated Press

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:48.519
reported on a “talking board” which was
all the rage at spiritualist gatherings across

00:03:48.519 --> 00:03:49.970
Ohio at the time.

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:55.700
The article went viral – or whatever the
Victorian version of “viral” was -- and

00:03:55.700 --> 00:04:03.360
one man who read it, Charles Kennard, of Baltimore,
Maryland, recognised an opportunity to capitalise

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:04.480
on the buzz.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:11.750
In 1890, he formed the Kennard Novelty Company
along with local patent attorney and inventor

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:17.400
Elijah Bond, and soon enough they were developing
the board for commercial use.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:24.580
Fun fact, if you visit Elijah Bond’s gravestone
you’ll notice it has a Ouija board on its

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:25.659
rear.

00:04:25.659 --> 00:04:28.870
But what could they call this bizarre new
product?

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:34.990
It’s commonly believed the name “Ouija”
comes from the French and German words for

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:38.419
“Yes” -- “Oui” and “Ja”.

00:04:38.419 --> 00:04:43.520
Elijah Bond, however, gives us a much creepier
explanation.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:48.949
Hissister-in-law, Helen Peters, was a shareholder
in the fledgling company, but she also happened

00:04:48.949 --> 00:04:49.949
to be a medium.

00:04:49.949 --> 00:04:55.150
And as far as she was concerned, if anyone
could come up with a good name for the board,

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:57.169
it was the board itself.

00:04:57.169 --> 00:05:03.800
So they set up a cosy little seance and asked
the ‘talking board’ what it wanted to

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:08.199
be called, to which it replied“Ouija”..

00:05:08.199 --> 00:05:13.729
The name is almost iconic today, but at the
time Elijah and Helen weren’t so sure.

00:05:13.729 --> 00:05:18.259
That’s a lot of vowels for one little word,
after all.

00:05:18.259 --> 00:05:24.889
Confused, they asked the board what it meant,
and, kind of ominously, it simply replied

00:05:24.889 --> 00:05:26.999
‘good luck’.

00:05:26.999 --> 00:05:31.780
Still, the name had clearly come from the
other side, and who were they to doubt the

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:33.550
wisdom of the spirits...

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:35.419
Or had it?

00:05:35.419 --> 00:05:41.129
It turns out Helen Peters was wearing a locket
at the time containing a photograph of a woman

00:05:41.129 --> 00:05:48.610
called “Ouija” or possibly ‘Ouida’
... but that was obviously just a coincidence.

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:52.729
Whatever the origin of its name, Helen clearly
believed in the board’s power.

00:05:52.729 --> 00:05:58.330
When some civil war heirlooms went missing
from her home she asked the board to identify

00:05:58.330 --> 00:06:03.770
the culprit, and it singled out one of her
relatives immediately.

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:09.099
Her subsequent accusation effectively tore
the family apart, forcing Peters to sell all

00:06:09.099 --> 00:06:11.619
of her stock in the company.

00:06:11.619 --> 00:06:16.770
Until her dying day, when asked about the
Ouija Board she would mutter: “Don’t play

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:17.929
the Ouija Board.

00:06:17.929 --> 00:06:18.929
It lies!”.

00:06:18.929 --> 00:06:23.339
Not exactly the best slogan for marketing
purposes, but hey.

00:06:23.339 --> 00:06:29.349
Family arguments aside, the mysterious ‘talking
board’ now had a name, but when Elijah Bond

00:06:29.349 --> 00:06:35.460
tried to obtain a patent in order to protect
his investment, the chief patent officer demanded

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:40.849
a demonstration, asking the ouija board to
spell out his name.

00:06:40.849 --> 00:06:45.379
To his horror and amazement, it did exactly
that.

00:06:45.379 --> 00:06:47.869
The patent officer was shaken.

00:06:47.869 --> 00:06:52.969
This was clearly the kind of arcane knowledge
only the dead could obtain.

00:06:52.969 --> 00:06:58.770
Well, unless Bond already knew the patent
officer’s name … I guess.

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:04.550
Which he probably did, being a patent attorney
and all ... but let’s not split hairs.

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:10.560
The patent was granted on the tenth of February
1891, and that same month the first few advertisements

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:16.669
started appearing in newspapers: “Ouija,
the Wonderful Talking Board!”one advert

00:07:16.669 --> 00:07:21.409
proclaimed, promising customers “a link
between the known and the unknown”.

00:07:21.409 --> 00:07:27.869
And all for just one-dollar-fifty ... which
is a bargain for a bit of light necromancy

00:07:27.869 --> 00:07:29.479
if you ask me.

00:07:29.479 --> 00:07:36.110
Plenty of people agreed, and the Kennard Company
sold Ouija Boards by the truckload.

00:07:36.110 --> 00:07:43.580
By 1892, Kennard had opened a second factory
in Baltimore, two in New York, two in Chicago,

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:49.159
and one in London ... But as effective as
the adverts were, one thing drove Ouija Board

00:07:49.159 --> 00:07:55.770
sales like nothing else could: pure, unadulterated
misery.

00:07:55.770 --> 00:08:01.529
In the years following the horrors of World
War One, sales went through the roof and Ouija

00:08:01.529 --> 00:08:06.759
Board use became so normalised that Norman
Rockwell’s illustration of a young couple

00:08:06.759 --> 00:08:11.639
using one graced the front cover of the Saturday
Evening Post.

00:08:11.639 --> 00:08:16.869
It was a similar story a decade or so later
during the Great Depression.

00:08:16.869 --> 00:08:22.199
While most businesses were having a tough
time of it, Ouija Boards, under the new ownership

00:08:22.199 --> 00:08:25.999
of the Fuld Company, were selling like hotcakes.

00:08:25.999 --> 00:08:31.699
This conveyor belt of tragedy continued into
World War Two, and over a single five month

00:08:31.699 --> 00:08:40.169
period in 1944, one New York department store
sold 50,000 Ouija Boards.

00:08:40.169 --> 00:08:46.760
In 1966, Parker Brothers bought the rights
to the board, and in 1967 -- as ever more

00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:51.739
American troops headed to join the war in
Vietnam and race riots spread across the country

00:08:51.739 --> 00:08:57.329
– over two million boards were sold,even
more than Monopoly.

00:08:57.329 --> 00:09:02.900
People were clearly taking solace in the spirit
world at a time when the real world was turning

00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:04.699
into a big pile of shit.

00:09:04.699 --> 00:09:08.720
But in 1973, something changed.

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:14.329
“The Exorcist” was released, which scared
people so much they’d pass out in theatres,

00:09:14.329 --> 00:09:18.839
run out screaming, or simply throw up into
their date’s popcorn.

00:09:18.839 --> 00:09:24.009
It was a worldwide phenomenon, and almost
overnight the Ouija Board became the “go

00:09:24.009 --> 00:09:29.259
to” method of opening the gates of Hell
and joining the dark lord Satan for a cup

00:09:29.259 --> 00:09:30.750
of tea and a chat.

00:09:30.750 --> 00:09:35.519
The Exorcist is about a twelve-year-old girl
called Reagan who’s possessed by the demon

00:09:35.519 --> 00:09:38.069
“Pazuzu” after using a Ouija Board.

00:09:38.069 --> 00:09:43.360
But the story itself was actually inspired
by a thirteen-year-old boy known only by the

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:51.069
pseudonym Roland Doe , who in 1949, was believed
to have been possessed by evil spirits.

00:09:51.069 --> 00:09:57.290
OK, so Roland wasn’t able to rotate his
head 360 degrees and didn’t projectile-vomit

00:09:57.290 --> 00:10:04.180
pea soup everywhere, but red scratches did
appear on his skin and he spent a lot of time

00:10:04.180 --> 00:10:12.339
swearing at passing priests in a loud, guttural
voice.

00:10:12.339 --> 00:10:16.790
According to the story, Roland Doe was just
a normal little boy until he was shown how

00:10:16.790 --> 00:10:20.110
to use a Ouija Board by his aunt.

00:10:20.110 --> 00:10:24.950
The success of The Exorcist actually started
a couple of years before the movie with William

00:10:24.950 --> 00:10:27.440
Peter Blatty’s bestselling novel.

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:32.310
Blatty had read the unofficial diaries kept
by Roland Doe’s priests and, during the

00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:37.120
writing process, became convinced that demonic
possession was real.

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:41.850
According to Blatty himself, he became obsessed
with the Ouija Board over a period of ten

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:46.940
days ... and then weird things started to
happen.

00:10:46.940 --> 00:10:51.699
His telephone suddenly jumped off the hook
when it rang, and his electric typewriter

00:10:51.699 --> 00:10:57.810
wrote a line of gibberish - things Blatty
thought might be “poltergeist activity”.

00:10:57.810 --> 00:11:05.940
Slowly but surely, the Ouija Board’s reputation
as something not just inexplicable but dangerous,

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:07.660
began to grow.

00:11:07.660 --> 00:11:13.149
Soon after the release of The Exorcist, religious
groups began denouncing it as Satan’s preferred

00:11:13.149 --> 00:11:15.089
method of communication.

00:11:15.089 --> 00:11:19.579
Catholics in particular had beef with it,
and over the decades many bishops have called

00:11:19.579 --> 00:11:20.629
for it to be banned.

00:11:20.629 --> 00:11:23.040
And the Catholics aren’t the only ones.

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:30.910
To this day, the Wisconsin Evangelical Lutheran
Synod expressly forbids the use of Ouija Boards,

00:11:30.910 --> 00:11:33.899
considering it a violation of the Ten Commandments.

00:11:33.899 --> 00:11:39.610
Now, it’s a while since I went to Sunday
School ... but I don’t remember God telling

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:43.910
Moses: “Thou shalt not play with Hasbro
board games”.

00:11:43.910 --> 00:11:46.419
And if he did, I’m going to hell.

00:11:46.419 --> 00:11:50.180
Some churches aren’t content with simply
forbidding their congregations from having

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:51.590
a dabble, either.

00:11:51.590 --> 00:11:58.720
2001 saw a mass burning of Ouija Boards in
Alamogordo, New Mexico, by various fundamentalist

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:03.839
groups, who considered the children’s toys
to be“symbols of witchcraft”.

00:12:03.839 --> 00:12:08.600
Mind you, they did also burn Harry Potter
books while they were at it, so it’s possible

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:10.670
they were just nutters.

00:12:10.670 --> 00:12:15.990
I mean, if you think Ron Weasley is the epitome
of evil then imagine how you’re going to

00:12:15.990 --> 00:12:18.681
feel if you ever find yourself face to face
with the devil himself.

00:12:18.681 --> 00:12:25.019
In a classic case of kicking a harmless board
game while it’s down, even the spiritualists

00:12:25.019 --> 00:12:29.300
have come out to condemn the Ouija Board these
days, despite its origins.

00:12:29.300 --> 00:12:34.370
Although, let’s be honest, that might have
more to do with job security than anything

00:12:34.370 --> 00:12:35.400
else.

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:39.260
After all, if you can pick up a cheap Ouija
Board on Amazon the next time you need to

00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:48.389
speak to the dead, why would you give your
hard-earned cash to “Madame Beryl the Mystic”?

00:12:48.389 --> 00:12:53.579
The most recent public condemnation of the
Ouija Board came from another unexpected source.

00:12:53.579 --> 00:13:01.870
In October 2020, paranormal investigator Paul
Marsters took Poundland to task in several

00:13:01.870 --> 00:13:08.899
UK newspapers, expressing his concern that
they were selling Ouija Boards ... for a pound.

00:13:08.899 --> 00:13:12.509
I mean, the clue is kind of in the name Paul.

00:13:12.509 --> 00:13:15.129
Some investigator you must be.

00:13:15.129 --> 00:13:20.379
Marsters warned that Ouija Boards should only
be used by those with proper training, and

00:13:20.379 --> 00:13:25.139
that all Hell would break loose - literally
- if any old mug was allowed to have a go

00:13:25.139 --> 00:13:26.139
with one.

00:13:26.139 --> 00:13:30.491
It isn’t clear where one would go to get
“proper training” in the use of a Ouija

00:13:30.491 --> 00:13:36.310
Board, but on my last check none of the major
British universities were offering anything

00:13:36.310 --> 00:13:41.839
relevant, so it seems we may have uncovered
a major blind spot in the education system

00:13:41.839 --> 00:13:42.839
there.

00:13:42.839 --> 00:13:46.689
As you might expect, the scientific community
has been calling “bullshit” on all of

00:13:46.689 --> 00:13:48.350
this stuff for years.

00:13:48.350 --> 00:13:53.820
According to those demon-denying party poopers,
the Ouija Board phenomenon is simply the result

00:13:53.820 --> 00:13:59.490
of the “Ideomotor Effect”, where the planchette
is simply guided by the unconscious muscular

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:01.560
movements of participants.

00:14:01.560 --> 00:14:07.329
The effect was first described by William
Carpenter back in 1852 while investigating

00:14:07.329 --> 00:14:13.329
the popular spiritualist fads like table turning,
and more recent experiments have supported

00:14:13.329 --> 00:14:14.740
his findings.

00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:22.040
In 2003, Professor Terence Hines showed that
muscular exertions guiding a planchette were

00:14:22.040 --> 00:14:27.689
enough to create an extremely powerful illusion
that spirits were truly at work.

00:14:27.689 --> 00:14:33.329
His experiments also suggested that the “messages”
received were related to whatever was going

00:14:33.329 --> 00:14:39.810
through the subject’s mind at the time ... so
if you ever receive a message saying “Subscribe

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:41.999
to Thoughty2” ... or “Buy this book!

00:14:41.999 --> 00:14:46.680
Now available on Amazon!” ... then it’s
best to just give in to what your unconscious

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:48.269
really wants.

00:14:48.269 --> 00:14:53.839
The ideomotor effect has also been used to
explain “dowsing” – the process of finding

00:14:53.839 --> 00:15:00.830
groundwater by walking around a field brandishing
a Y-shaped stick ... or an L-shaped rod, if

00:15:00.830 --> 00:15:02.939
you’re one of those “modern” dowser
types.

00:15:02.939 --> 00:15:07.810
You won’t be surprised to hear there’s
no actual evidence to support this pseudoscience,

00:15:07.810 --> 00:15:12.490
and that all scientific experiments into the
subject have shown that walking around with

00:15:12.490 --> 00:15:18.209
a dowsing rod looking like a prat gives you
no better chance of finding groundwater than

00:15:18.209 --> 00:15:19.329
anyone else.

00:15:19.329 --> 00:15:26.340
What’s really bizarre about dowsing, however,
is that as recently as 2017, many UK water

00:15:26.340 --> 00:15:32.209
companies were reportedly using “dowsers”
to find burst pipes.

00:15:32.209 --> 00:15:37.119
At least according to science writer Sally
Le Page, although the companies in question

00:15:37.119 --> 00:15:42.329
soon denied it ... probably because they’d
still like to be viewed as trusted national

00:15:42.329 --> 00:15:48.330
utility providers, and not as a coven of witches
ready to be burnt at the stake.

00:15:48.330 --> 00:15:51.709
PR departments hate it when that kind of thing
happens.

00:15:51.709 --> 00:15:57.399
So, yes, scientists are generally sceptical
about the Ouija Board as a means of contacting

00:15:57.399 --> 00:16:02.629
the dead - or anyone else, for that matter,
but that doesn’t make the fact it will reliably

00:16:02.629 --> 00:16:06.720
spell out messages when used any less fascinating.

00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:11.550
Researchers at the University of British Columbia
have used Ouija boards to examine how the

00:16:11.550 --> 00:16:17.769
mind processes information and have even conducted
experiments with a Ouija-playing robot.

00:16:17.769 --> 00:16:23.939
The results suggest the unconscious mind is
a lot smarter than anyone knew.

00:16:23.939 --> 00:16:29.189
But while scientists are only now beginning
to understand the various ways the Ouija Board

00:16:29.189 --> 00:16:34.399
might tap into the subconscious, creative
types have been taking advantage of the phenomenon

00:16:34.399 --> 00:16:35.430
for years.

00:16:35.430 --> 00:16:41.889
Pulitzer Prize winner James Merrill used a
Ouija board to write his epic poem “The

00:16:41.889 --> 00:16:47.560
Changing Light at Sandover”; G.K Chesterton
-- author of the “Father Brown” mysteries

00:16:47.560 --> 00:16:54.129
-- used a Ouija Board for inspiration in his
teenage years, and some of Yeats’s later

00:16:54.129 --> 00:16:59.480
poetry was inspired by Ouija messages his
wife received.

00:16:59.480 --> 00:17:06.720
One of the most well-known examples of Ouija
writing came in 1917, when Emily Grant Hutchings

00:17:06.720 --> 00:17:13.780
claimed her book “Jap Herron” was written
by the one and only Mark Twain.

00:17:13.780 --> 00:17:18.250
Despite the factTwain had already been dead
for seven years at the time, Hutchings was

00:17:18.250 --> 00:17:23.981
convinced the two of them had had many long
and fascinating conversations via the Ouija

00:17:23.981 --> 00:17:25.470
Board.

00:17:25.470 --> 00:17:31.520
Until Twain’s daughter Olivia sued her,
that is, at which point she changed her mind

00:17:31.520 --> 00:17:33.090
pretty quickly.

00:17:33.090 --> 00:17:34.090
Funny that.

00:17:34.090 --> 00:17:40.010
And then there’s Vincent Furnier – who
you probably know as Alice Cooper -- who,

00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:45.899
according to some at least, took his unusual
stage name from a Ouija Board after it told

00:17:45.899 --> 00:17:50.630
him he was the reincarnation of a seventeenth
century witch.

00:17:50.630 --> 00:17:55.770
These days, the Ouija Board pops up most frequently
as a plot device in a whole bunch of horror

00:17:55.770 --> 00:17:56.770
movies.

00:17:56.770 --> 00:18:01.650
There’s “What Lies Beneath”, “The
Conjuring 2”, “Paranormal Activity 1 and

00:18:01.650 --> 00:18:06.899
2” and, of course, 2014’s “Ouija”
and its prequel “Origin of Evil”.

00:18:06.899 --> 00:18:12.809
Hasbro -- who bought the rights from Parker
Brothers in 1991 -- partially funded the movie,

00:18:12.809 --> 00:18:17.740
so there must have been quite a lot of backslapping
when sales of the board went up 300 percent

00:18:17.740 --> 00:18:18.740
in the aftermath.

00:18:18.740 --> 00:18:23.790
You never know, perhaps even a few prayers
of thanks were offered up… or more likely

00:18:23.790 --> 00:18:25.179
down.

00:18:25.179 --> 00:18:30.039
To this day, there’s still no sign of our
fascination with the Ouija Board slowing down.

00:18:30.039 --> 00:18:34.740
And whether you’re a believer or a sceptic,
it’s hard to deny there’s a hint of danger

00:18:34.740 --> 00:18:40.320
about it; that by using a Ouija Board you’re
dipping your toe into the murky waters of

00:18:40.320 --> 00:18:41.320
the unknown.

00:18:41.320 --> 00:18:47.110
There are even a variety of Ouija apps you
can download if you prefer your demon-summoning

00:18:47.110 --> 00:18:53.309
to be a bit more “twenty-first century”
... although, word of advice, you shouldn’t

00:18:53.309 --> 00:18:58.970
respond to their messages with gifs, emojis,
or the phrase “U OK Hun?”

00:18:58.970 --> 00:19:01.360
They absolutely hate that.

00:19:01.360 --> 00:19:11.820
Thanks for watching.

